@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "ORDER BY Clause";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY Clause
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The ORDER BY clause is used to sort the result of a SQL
            query in ascending or descending order.
        </p>

        <p>
            By default, ORDER BY sorts data in ascending order.
            We can also use the <strong>DESC</strong> keyword to sort
            the data in descending order.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS ORDER BY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is ORDER BY Clause?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>ORDER BY</strong> clause is used to arrange
            records returned by a SELECT query according to one or
            more columns.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is useful when we want to display data in a specific
            sequence such as lowest to highest, highest to lowest,
            A to Z or Z to A.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name
ORDER BY column_name;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                        <th>Fees</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>35000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                        <td>40000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                        <td>30000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>24</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>45000</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ASCENDING ORDER -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY ASC
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>ASC</strong> keyword sorts the records in
            ascending order.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age ASC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The youngest student will appear first and the oldest
            student will appear last.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DESCENDING ORDER -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY DESC
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DESC</strong> keyword sorts the records in
            descending order.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The oldest student will appear first and the youngest
            student will appear last.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DEFAULT ASCENDING -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Default Sorting
        </h2>

        <p>
            If we do not specify ASC or DESC, SQL Server uses
            ascending order by default.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The above query is equivalent to using
            <strong>ORDER BY Age ASC</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY STRING -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY with Character Data
        </h2>

        <p>
            ORDER BY can also be used with character or string
            columns.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY StudentName ASC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This sorts the student names alphabetically.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY FEES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY with Fees
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY Fees DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This displays the student having the highest fees first.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- MULTIPLE COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY Multiple Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can sort records using more than one column.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY City ASC, Age DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            First, records are sorted by City in ascending order.
            Within the same city, records are sorted by Age in
            descending order.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY WITH WHERE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY with WHERE
        </h2>

        <p>
            ORDER BY can be combined with WHERE to filter records
            and then sort the filtered result.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune'
ORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query first selects students from Pune and then
            sorts them by age from highest to lowest.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY COLUMN POSITION -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ORDER BY using Column Position
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server also allows sorting using the position of a
            column in the SELECT list.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT StudentName, Age, City
FROM Students
ORDER BY 2 DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Here, <strong>2</strong> represents the second column
            selected, which is Age.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ORDER BY is used to sort query results.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ASC sorts data in ascending order.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DESC sorts data in descending order.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ASC is the default sorting order.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Multiple columns can be used with ORDER BY.
                </li>

                <li>
                    ORDER BY can be combined with WHERE.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the ORDER BY clause
                is used to sort SQL Server query results. We covered
                ascending and descending order, sorting by multiple
                columns, combining ORDER BY with WHERE, and sorting
                using column position.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


